# Rust build artefacts.
target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Benchmark and simulation intermediates.
#
# What is *kept* is the result: `benchmarks/results/results.jsonl` (one row per
# dataset and arm), `benchmarks/results/gates.json`, the Rprof summary, and the
# simulation summaries with their gzipped per-replicate rows. What is dropped is
# the per-arm scratch: a full surface writes tens of megabytes of raw run
# directories and the simulation writes one directory per replicate, none of
# which is an input to anything.
benchmarks/results/run/
sim-data/

# Generated benchmark datasets: `bm5` alone is 100 million cells. They are a
# pure function of `scripts/make_bench_data.py` plus its seed, and regenerating
# them is one `make bench-data`.
benchmarks/datasets/

# The R wrapper's build products. `make r-install` rebuilds them.
r/ancombc2rs/src/*.so
r/ancombc2rs/src/*.o
r/ancombc2rs/src/*.dll
r/ancombc2rs/src/.ancombc2_rs_built
.rlib/

# The full simulation grid's per-replicate rows. One cell is 1000 replicates of
# two arms, and the full grid is 252 cells: `validation/simulation/results` runs to
# 54 GB, of which the sharded R arms are the bulk. None of it is an input to
# anything -- `ancombc2-sim summarise` reduces it to the `*.summary.json` files,
# and those *are* committed, because they are the Layer 3 acceptance evidence.
#
# What is dropped is the raw rows; what is kept is the reduction of them.
validation/simulation/results/*.jsonl
validation/simulation/results/*.part*.jsonl
validation/simulation/results/*.shard*.jsonl
validation/simulation/probe/

# The full grid's working data set, as opposed to the quick grid's `sim-data/`.
# Same reasoning as the line above: regenerated by the grid runner, and 33 GB.
sim-data-full/
